  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 300 PM EDT Mon Jul 27 2026\n\nCurrently-Tonight... KMLB radar imagery shows isolated to widely\nscattered generally south-southeast moving showers and lighting \nstorms along, as well as north of I-4. GOES-16 satellite imagery \nand local observations shows partly to mostly cloudy skies. \nAnalysis charts show low pressure (1010mb) over the southwest \nAtlantic with a stationary boundary that extends from the low \nacross the southwest Atlantic and northern Florida. Current \ntemperatures are in the mid 80s to low 90s with dew points in the \nmid 70s to around 80 degrees, in addition to heat index values \ngenerally up to 105-112F degrees. The highest heat index values \nare across northern ECFL where a Heat Advisory is in effect until \n8PM. \n\nScattered to numerous generally southeast moving showers (60-70% \nchance) and lightning storms are forecast to increase in coverage \nthis afternoon and evening as collisions occur between west- \nsouthwest flow, the east coast sea breeze, and outflow from \nprevious showers/lightning storms. Rain chances gradually reduce \nafter 7pm. The potential exists for isolated strong storms. The \nmain hazards will be frequent lightning, wind gusts up to \n40-50mph, and heavy rainfall (1-3\" with a 1 in 10 chance in excess\nof 3\" up to 4-5\"). WSW winds at 5-10mph are expected this \nafternoon with winds backing onshore into this evening. Lows in \nthe mid 70s to near 80F degrees are forecast. \n\nTuesday-Wednesday... Rain chances will trend lower tomorrow\n(40-60%) and especially on Wednesday with drier air due to \nincreasing offshore flow. However, a similar forecast to today is \nexpected on Tuesday as the aforementioned stationary\nboundary sags southwards coupled with PWATs between 2.0-2.4.\" \nRain chances decrease further on Wednesday with isolated to \nscattered showers (20-40% chance) forecast (variable storm \nmotions) to develop in the afternoon and evening hours. The main \nhazards will be frequent lightning, wind gusts up to 40-50mph, and\nheavy rainfall (1-3\" with a 1 in 10 chance in excess of 3\" up to \n4-5\" on Tuesday and 1-2\" with a 1 in 10 chance in excess of 2\" up \nto 3\" on Wednesday). Afternoon highs in the low to mid 90s are \nforecast with heat index values up to 105-112F degrees (highest \nacross N ECFL on Tuesday and across all of ECFL on Wednesday). \nAbove normal (1-4F+) lows in the mid 70s to around 80F degrees are\nforecast. A Moderate to Major HeatRisk is expected with portions \nof Lake county in an Extreme Heat Risk. This level of heat affects\nanyone without adequate cooling and hydration. Remember to \npractice heat safety such as wearing light clothing, shifting \noutdoor work away from the hours of 10AM-4PM, drink plenty of \nfluids, and take frequent breaks from the heat. \n\nThursday-Sunday... Rain chances increase late week and remain\nhigh through at least Saturday as a stationary boundary stalls\nover the southern US. The potential exists for isolated strong to\nmarginally severe storms Thursday-Saturday. However, the trends \nwith the NBM have been a lowering in the POPs in the extended \nforecast and MOS data suggests that the probability of \nprecipitation are a little too high. Scattered to widespread \nshowers (50-90% highest across N ECFL) and lightning storms are \nforecast on Thursday with scattered to numerous showers and \nlightning storms forecast Friday, as well as Saturday with \ndecreasing rain chances on Sunday. There is a Marginal Risk for \nExcessive Rainfall (5-14% risk) for much of ECFL on Thursday and \nFriday. The heat decreases with the higher rain chances with \nafternoon highs in the upper 80s to low 90s forecast.",
